"CSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2529view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"lineno\":1720}); Total lines: 2555\nL1690: The 【115†if()】 function entirely fails to parse according to its 【521†argument grammar】: there’s no `:` character separating the test from the value in the first branch.\nL1691: \nL1692: To get the desired behavior of \"spreading\" the variable into the function’s arguments, use ...':\nL1693: \nL1694:     .valid-if {\nL1695:       --if-clause: media(width >= 600px): blue;\nL1696:       color: if(...var(--if-clause); else: green;);\nL1697:     }\nL1698: The 【866†spread syntax】 is three distinct 【157†<delim-token>】s with the value `\".\"`, all of which must not contain any whitespace between them, or between the group and the subsequent 【126†arbitrary substitution function】.\nL1699: \nL1700: That is, ...var(--foo) is a valid use of the 【866†spread syntax】, but ... var(--foo) is not, nor is . . .var(--foo).\nL1701: The latter usages will result in the 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 being evaluated at the normal time, after the 【521†argument grammar】 has been applied, and the period characters being part of the function’s value.\nL1702: Note: The 【866†spread syntax】 is only used within an 【126†arbitrary substitution function’s】 value, as it’s only referenced by the 【171†substitution】 algorithm when parsing an arbitrary substitution function. Using it outside of that, such as in 【690†width: ...var(--sidebar-width);】, is not recognized as an early invocation; instead, the periods are just part of the property’s value, unrelated to the 【127†var()】 function, and would in this case make the width property invalid.\nL1703: (This is similar to JavaScript, where this syntax was borrowed from, where `[1, ...arr, 5]` is valid, but `var x = ...arr;` is not.)\nL1704: To substitute early-invoked functions in a sequence of 【168†component values】 `values`:\nL1705: \nL1706:   1. 【526†For each†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 `func` in `values` (ordered via a depth-first pre-order traversal) using the 【866†spread syntax】 that is not nested in the contents of another arbitrary substitution function:\nL1707: \nL1708:     1. 【863†Substitute early-invoked functions】 in `func`’s contents, and let `early result` be the result.\nL1709:     2. If `early result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1710: \nL1711:     3. 【160†Parse】 `early result` acccording to `func`’s 【521†argument grammar】. If this returns failure, replace `func` in values with the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】 and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】; otherwise, let `arguments` be the result.\nL1712:     4. 【525†Replace an arbitrary substitution function】 for `func`, given `arguments`, as defined by that function. Let `result` be the returned list of 【168†component values】.\nL1713: \nL1714:     5. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value. Otherwise, replace `func` in `values` with `result`.\nL1715: \nL1716:   2. Return `values`.\nL1717: ### Resolving in Properties\nL1718: \nL1719: Unless otherwise specified, 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 can be used in place of any part of any property’s value (including within other 【114†functional notations】); and are not valid in any other context.\nL1720: \nL1721: Should any of these functions be valid in contexts outside of properties?\nL1722: \nL1723: Tests\nL1724:   * 【867†variable-external-font-face-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【868†(live test)†wpt.live】 【869†(source)†github.com】\nL1725:   * 【870†variable-font-face-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【871†(live test)†wpt.live】 【872†(source)†github.com】\nL1726:   * 【873†variable-font-face-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【874†(live test)†wpt.live】 【875†(source)†github.com】\nL1727: \nL1728: For example, the following code incorrectly attempts to use a variable as a property name:\nL1729: \nL1730:     .foo {\nL1731:       --side: margin-top;\nL1732:       var(--side): 20px;\nL1733:     }\nL1734: This is not equivalent to setting 【776†margin-top: 20px;】. Instead, the second declaration is simply thrown away as a syntax error for having an invalid property name.\nL1735: \nL1736: If a property value contains one or more 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】, and all of those functions are themselves syntactically valid according to their 【521†argument grammar】s, the entire value’s grammar must be assumed to be valid at parse time.\nL1737: \nL1738: Tests\nL1739:   * 【876†variable-reference-18.html†wpt.fyi】 【877†(live test)†wpt.live】 【878†(source)†github.com】\nL1740:   * 【879†variable-reference-19.html†wpt.fyi】 【880†(live test)†wpt.live】 【881†(source)†github.com】\nL1741:   * 【882†variable-reference-30.html†wpt.fyi】 【883†(live test)†wpt.live】 【884†(source)†github.com】\nL1742: 【126†Arbitrary substitution functions】 are 【171†substituted】 during style 【173†computation】, before any other value transformations or introspection can occur. If a property, after 【531†property replacement】, does not match its declared grammar, the declaration is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】.\nL1743: Note: Since 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 resolve at 【173†computed value】 time, if the resulting value after substitution is invalid, the property falls back (essentially) to 【885†unset】 behavior, rather than falling back to an earlier value in the 【482†cascade】 the way declarations invalid at parse time do. See 【80†Invalid Substitution】.\nL1744: \nL1745: Tests\nL1746:   * 【886†variable-declaration-16.html†wpt.fyi】 【887†(live test)†wpt.live】 【888†(source)†github.com】\nL1747:   * 【889†variable-declaration-17.html†wpt.fyi】 【890†(live test)†wpt.live】 【891†(source)†github.com】\nL1748:   * 【892†variable-declaration-18.html†wpt.fyi】 【893†(live test)†wpt.live】 【894†(source)†github.com】\nL1749:   * 【895†variable-declaration-19.html†wpt.fyi】 【896†(live test)†wpt.live】 【897†(source)†github.com】\nL1750:   * 【898†variable-declaration-21.html†wpt.fyi】 【899†(live test)†wpt.live】 【900†(source)†github.com】\nL1751:   * 【901†variable-transitions-transition-property-all-before-value.html†wpt.fyi】 【902†(live test)†wpt.live】 【903†(source)†github.com】\nL1752:   * 【904†variable-transitions-value-before-transition-property-all.html†wpt.fyi】 【905†(live test)†wpt.live】 【906†(source)†github.com】\nL1753: If a property value, after 【531†property replacement】, contains only a single 【484†CSS-wide keyword】 (and possibly whitespace/comments), its value is determined as if that keyword were its 【393†specified value】 all along.\nL1754: \nL1755: Tests\nL1756:   * 【907†whitespace-in-fallback-crash.html†wpt.fyi】 【908†(live test)†wpt.live】 【909†(source)†github.com】\nL1757:   * 【910†wide-keyword-fallback-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【911†(live test)†wpt.live】 【912†(source)†github.com】\nL1758:   * 【913†wide-keyword-fallback-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【914†(live test)†wpt.live】 【915†(source)†github.com】\nL1759: For example, the following usage is fine from a syntax standpoint, but results in nonsense when the variable is substituted in:\nL1760: \nL1761:     :root { --looks-valid: 20px; }\nL1762:     p { background-color: var(--looks-valid); }\nL1763: \nL1764: \nL1765: Since 20px is an invalid value for 【916†background-color】, the property becomes 【486†invalid at computed-value time】, and instead resolves to 【917†transparent】 (the 【918†initial value】 for background-color).\nL1766: If the property was one that’s inherited by default, such as 【919†color】, it would compute to the inherited value rather than the initial value.\nL1767: \nL1768: While a 【127†var()】 function can’t get a 【484†CSS-wide keyword】 from the 【542†custom property】 itself—​if you tried to specify that, like --foo: initial;, it would just trigger 【920†explicit defaulting】 for the custom property—​it can have a CSS-wide keyword in its fallback:\nL1769: \nL1770:     p { color: var(--does-not-exist, initial); }\nL1771: In the above code, if the --does-not-exist property didn’t exist or is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】, the 【127†var()】 will instead substitute in the 【921†initial】 keyword, making the property behave as if it was originally 【919†color: initial】. This will make it take on the document’s initial color value, rather than defaulting to inheritance, as it would if there were no fallback.\nL1772: \nL1773: To replace substitution functions in a property `prop`:\nL1774:   1. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `prop`’s value, given «\"property\", `prop`’s name» as the 【529†substitution context】. Let `result` be the returned 【168†component value】 sequence.\nL1775: \nL1776:   2. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1777: \nL1778:   3. 【160†Parse】 `result` according to `prop`’s grammar. If this returns failure, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1779:   4. Otherwise, replace `prop`’s value with the parsed result.\nL1780: \nL1781: Tests\nL1782:   * 【922†css-variable-change-style-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【923†(live test)†wpt.live】 【924†(source)†github.com】\nL1783:   * 【925†css-variable-change-style-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【926†(live test)†wpt.live】 【927†(source)†github.com】\nL1784:   * 【928†variable-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【929†(live test)†wpt.live】 【930†(source)†github.com】\nL1785:   * 【931†variable-declaration-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【932†(live test)†wpt.live】 【933†(source)†github.com】\nL1786:   * 【934†variable-declaration-03.html†wpt.fyi】 【935†(live test)†wpt.live】 【936†(source)†github.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2529view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"lineno\":940}); Total lines: 2555\nL910: Like the 【538†inherit】 keyword, the inherit() 【114†functional notation】 resolves to the 【173†computed value】 of a property on the parent. Rather than resolving to the value of the same property, however, it resolves to a sequence of 【168†component values】 representing the computed value of the property specified as its first argument. Its second argument, if present, is used as a fallback in case the first argument resolves to the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL911: 【545†inherit()】 is an 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 whose syntax is defined as:\nL912: \nL913:     【545†<inherit()>】 = inherit( 【555†<custom-property-name>】【411†,】 【117†<declaration-value>】【147†?】 )\nL914: \nL915: \nL916: The 【545†inherit()】 function’s 【521†argument grammar】 is:\nL917: \nL918:     【556†<inherit-args>】 = inherit( 【117†<declaration-value>】【411†,】 【117†<declaration-value>】【147†?】 )\nL919: Like 【127†var()】, a bare comma can be used with nothing following it, indicating that the second 【117†<declaration-value>】 was passed, just as an empty sequence.\nL920: \nL921: Note: That is, inherit(--foo) does not pass a fallback value, but inherit(--foo,) does (the fallback is just empty).\nL922: \nL923: To 【525†replace an inherit() function】, given a list of `arguments`:\nL924:   1. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in the first 【117†<declaration-value>】 of `arguments`, then 【160†parse】 it as a 【555†<custom-property-name>】.\nL925: \nL926:   2. If parsing returned a 【555†<custom-property-name>】, and the 【537†inherited value】 of that 【542†custom property】 on the element does not contain the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, return that inherited value.\nL927:   3. Otherwise, if a second 【117†<declaration-value>】? was passed in `arguments`, 【171†substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in that argument, and return the result.\nL928: \nL929:   4. Otherwise, return the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL930: Note: Future levels of CSS may allow specifying standard CSS properties in 【545†inherit()】; however because the tokenization of 【173†computed values】 is not fully standardized for all CSS properties, this feature is deferred from Level 5. Note that the computed value differs from the 【435†used value】, and is not always the resolved value returned by `【557†getComputedStyle()】`; thus even if inherit(width) were allowed, it would frequently return the keyword 【558†auto】, not the used 【155†<length>】.\nL931: ### 8.7. Attribute References: the 【138†attr()】 notation\nL932: The attr() function substitutes the value of an 【559†attribute†dom.spec.whatwg.org】 on an 【560†element†dom.spec.whatwg.org】 into a property, similar to how the 【127†var()】 function substitutes a 【542†custom property】 value into a function.\nL933: \nL934:     attr() = attr( 【561†<attr-name>】 【562†<attr-type>】【147†?】 【411†,】 【117†<declaration-value>】【147†?】)\nL935: \nL936:     <attr-name> = [ 【563†<ident-token>】【147†?】 '|' ]【147†?】 【563†<ident-token>】\nL937:     <attr-type> = type( 【137†<syntax>】 ) 【133†|】 raw-string 【133†|】 number 【133†|】 【564†<attr-unit>】\nL938:     <attr-unit> = 【565†<custom-ident>】\nL939: The 【138†attr()】 function’s 【521†argument grammar】 is:\nL940: \nL941:     【566†<attr-args>】 = attr( 【117†<declaration-value>】【411†,】 【117†<declaration-value>】【147†?】 )\nL942: \nL943: \nL944: Tests\nL945:   * 【567†attr-IACVT.html†wpt.fyi】 【568†(live test)†wpt.live】 【569†(source)†github.com】\nL946:   * 【570†attr-all-types.html†wpt.fyi】 【571†(live test)†wpt.live】 【572†(source)†github.com】\nL947:   * 【573†attr-argument-grammar.html†wpt.fyi】 【574†(live test)†wpt.live】 【575†(source)†github.com】\nL948:   * 【576†attr-color-invalid-cast.html†wpt.fyi】 【577†(live test)†wpt.live】 【578†(source)†github.com】\nL949:   * 【579†attr-color-valid.html†wpt.fyi】 【580†(live test)†wpt.live】 【581†(source)†github.com】\nL950:   * 【582†attr-container-style-query.html†wpt.fyi】 【583†(live test)†wpt.live】 【584†(source)†github.com】\nL951:   * 【585†attr-crash.html†wpt.fyi】 【586†(live test)†wpt.live】 【587†(source)†github.com】\nL952:   * 【588†attr-css-wide-keywords.html†wpt.fyi】 【589†(live test)†wpt.live】 【590†(source)†github.com】\nL953:   * 【591†attr-cycle.html†wpt.fyi】 【592†(live test)†wpt.live】 【593†(source)†github.com】\nL954:   * 【594†attr-dynamic-marker-content.html†wpt.fyi】 【595†(live test)†wpt.live】 【596†(source)†github.com】\nL955:   * 【597†attr-in-max.html†wpt.fyi】 【598†(live test)†wpt.live】 【599†(source)†github.com】\nL956:   * 【600†attr-in-slotted.html†wpt.fyi】 【601†(live test)†wpt.live】 【602†(source)†github.com】\nL957:   * 【603†attr-invalidation.html†wpt.fyi】 【604†(live test)†wpt.live】 【605†(source)†github.com】\nL958:   * 【606†attr-length-invalid-cast.html†wpt.fyi】 【607†(live test)†wpt.live】 【608†(source)†github.com】\nL959:   * 【609†attr-length-specified.html†wpt.fyi】 【610†(live test)†wpt.live】 【611†(source)†github.com】\nL960:   * 【612†attr-length-valid-zero-nofallback.html†wpt.fyi】 【613†(live test)†wpt.live】 【614†(source)†github.com】\nL961:   * 【615†attr-length-valid-zero.html†wpt.fyi】 【616†(live test)†wpt.live】 【617†(source)†github.com】\nL962:   * 【618†attr-length-valid.html†wpt.fyi】 【619†(live test)†wpt.live】 【620†(source)†github.com】\nL963:   * 【621†attr-namespace-case-sensitivity.xhtml†wpt.fyi】 【622†(live test)†wpt.live】 【623†(source)†github.com】\nL964:   * 【624†attr-namespace-non-existing.html†wpt.fyi】 【625†(live test)†wpt.live】 【626†(source)†github.com】\nL965:   * 【627†attr-namespace-valid.xhtml†wpt.fyi】 【628†(live test)†wpt.live】 【629†(source)†github.com】\nL966:   * 【630†attr-namespace-wildcard.html†wpt.fyi】 【631†(live test)†wpt.live】 【632†(source)†github.com】\nL967:   * 【633†attr-notype-fallback.html†wpt.fyi】 【634†(live test)†wpt.live】 【635†(source)†github.com】\nL968:   * 【636†attr-null-namespace.xhtml†wpt.fyi】 【637†(live test)†wpt.live】 【638†(source)†github.com】\nL969:   * 【639†attr-pseudo-elem-invalidation-2.html†wpt.fyi】 【640†(live test)†wpt.live】 【641†(source)†github.com】\nL970:   * 【642†attr-pseudo-elem-invalidation.html†wpt.fyi】 【643†(live test)†wpt.live】 【644†(source)†github.com】\nL971:   * 【645†attr-pseudo-element-marker.html†wpt.fyi】 【646†(live test)†wpt.live】 【647†(source)†github.com】\nL972:   * 【648†attr-pseudo-element-originating.html†wpt.fyi】 【649†(live test)†wpt.live】 【650†(source)†github.com】\nL973:   * 【651†attr-pseudo-element-placeholder.html†wpt.fyi】 【652†(live test)†wpt.live】 【653†(source)†github.com】\nL974:   * 【654†attr-revert-rule.html†wpt.fyi】 【655†(live test)†wpt.live】 【656†(source)†github.com】\nL975:   * 【657†attr-serialization.html†wpt.fyi】 【658†(live test)†wpt.live】 【659†(source)†github.com】\nL976:   * 【660†attr-style-sharing-1.html†wpt.fyi】 【661†(live test)†wpt.live】 【662†(source)†github.com】\nL977:   * 【663†attr-style-sharing-2.html†wpt.fyi】 【664†(live test)†wpt.live】 【665†(source)†github.com】\nL978:   * 【666†attr-style-sharing-3.html†wpt.fyi】 【667†(live test)†wpt.live】 【668†(source)†github.com】\nL979:   * 【669†attr-style-sharing-4.html†wpt.fyi】 【670†(live test)†wpt.live】 【671†(source)†github.com】\nL980:   * 【672†attr-style-sharing-5.html†wpt.fyi】 【673†(live test)†wpt.live】 【674†(source)†github.com】\nL981:   * 【675†attr-universal-selector.html†wpt.fyi】 【676†(live test)†wpt.live】 【677†(source)†github.com】\nL982:   * 【678†html-attr-case-insensitivity.html†wpt.fyi】 【679†(live test)†wpt.live】 【680†(source)†github.com】\nL983: Like 【127†var()】, a bare comma can be used with nothing following it, indicating that the second 【117†<declaration-value>】 was passed, just as an empty sequence.\nL984: \nL985: Note: That is, attr(foo) does not pass a fallback value, but attr(foo,) does (the fallback is just empty).\nL986: 【564†<attr-unit>】 is intended to represents an 【161†identifier】 that is an 【681†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 match for the name of a CSS dimension unit, such as 【682†px】, or the 【157†<delim-token>】 %. As this set expands regularly, it is actually specified as a 【565†<custom-ident>】, and triggers fallback behavior when it doesn’t match a known unit (rather than making the function invalid).\nL987: \nL988: The arguments of 【138†attr()】 are:\nL989: \nL990: 【561†<attr-name>】\nL991: Gives the name of the attribute being referenced, similar to <wq-name> (from 【683†[SELECTORS-3]】) but without the possibility of a wildcard prefix.\nL992: If no namespace is specified (just an identifier is given, like attr(foo)), the null namespace is implied. (This is usually what’s desired, as namespaced attributes are rare. In particular, HTML and SVG do not contain namespaced attributes.) Unless otherwise specified, the case-sensitivity/matching rules of 【561†<attr-name>】 are identical to 【684†attribute selectors】 (and thus might depend on the host language).\nL993: \nL994: Whitespace is not allowed between any of the components of 【561†<attr-name>】.\nL995: If 【138†attr()】 is used in a property applied to an element, it references the attribute of the given name on that element; if applied to a 【685†pseudo-element】, the attribute is looked up on the pseudo-element’s 【686†originating element】.\nL996: \nL997: 【562†<attr-type>】\nL998: \nL999: \nL1000: Specifies how the attribute value is 【160†parsed】 into a CSS value.\nL1001: If given as a 【687†type()】 function, the value is parsed according to the 【137†<syntax>】 argument, and substitutes as the resulting tokens. For this purpose, <url> is invalid as a 【145†<syntax-single-component>】. Values that fail to parse according to the syntax trigger fallback.\nL1002: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 2 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/)\nciteturn2529view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/#invalid-at-computed-value-time\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 829\nL0: # CSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 2\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Editor’s Draft†www.w3.org】, 9 October 2026\nL3: \nL4: More details about this document\nL5: This version:\nL6:     【1†https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/】\nL7: Implementation Report:\nL8:     【2†https://wpt.fyi/results/css/css-variables†wpt.fyi】\nL9: Issue Tracking:\nL10:     【3†CSSWG Issues Repository†github.com】\nL11: Editor:\nL12:     【4†Tab Atkins Jr.†xanthir.com】 (Google)\nL13: Suggest an Edit for this Spec:\nL14:     【5†GitHub Editor†github.com】\nL15: Test Suite:\nL16:     【6†https://wpt.fyi/results/css/css-variables/†wpt.fyi】\nL17: 【7†Copyright†www.w3.org】 © 2026 【8†World Wide Web Consortium†www.w3.org】. W3C (World Wide Web Consortium)^{®} 【9†liability†www.w3.org】, 【10†trademark†www.w3.org】 and 【11†permissive document license†www.w3.org】 rules apply.\nL18: \nL19: * * *\nL20: ## Abstract\nL21: \nL22: This module introduces cascading variables as a new primitive value type that is accepted by all CSS properties, and custom properties for defining them.\nL23: \nL24: 【12†CSS†www.w3.org】 is a language for describing the rendering of structured documents (such as HTML and XML) on screen, on paper, etc.\nL25: ## Status of this document\nL26: \nL27: This is a public copy of the editors’ draft. It is provided for discussion only and may change at any moment. Its publication here does not imply endorsement of its contents by W3C. Don’t cite this document other than as work in progress.\nL28: Please send feedback by 【13†filing issues in GitHub†github.com】 (preferred), including the spec code “css-variables” in the title, like this: “[css-variables] …summary of comment…”. All issues and comments are 【14†archived†lists.w3.org】. Alternately, feedback can be sent to the (【15†archived†lists.w3.org】) public mailing list www-style@w3.org.\nL29: \nL30: This document is governed by the 【16†18 August 2025 W3C Process Document†www.w3.org】.\nL31: ## Table of Contents\nL32:   1. 【17†1 Introduction】\nL33:     1. 【18†1.1 Value Definitions】\nL34:   2. 【19†2 Defining Custom Properties: the --* family of properties】\nL35:     1. 【20†2.1 Custom Property Value Syntax】\nL36:     2. 【21†2.2 Guaranteed-Invalid Values】\nL37:   3. 【22†3 Using Cascading Variables: the var() notation】\nL38:     1. 【23†3.1 Variable Units】\nL39:   4. 【24†4 APIs】\nL40:     1. 【25†4.1 Serializing Custom Properties】\nL41:   5. 【26†5 Changes】\nL42:     1. 【27†5.1 Changes Since the 16 June 2022 CR Snapshot】\nL43:     2. 【28†5.2 Changes Since the 11 November 2021 CR Draft】\nL44:     3. 【29†5.3 Changes Since the 03 December 2015 CR】\nL45:     4. 【30†5.4 Changes since the May 6 2014 Last Call Working Draft】\nL46:   6. 【31†6 Acknowledgments】\nL47:   7. 【32†7 Privacy Considerations】\nL48:   8. 【33†8 Security Considerations】\nL49:   9. 【34†Conformance】\nL50:     1. 【35†Document conventions】\nL51:     2. 【36†Conformance classes】\nL52:     3. 【37†Partial implementations】\nL53:       1. 【38†Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features】\nL54:     4. 【39†Non-experimental implementations】\nL55:   10. 【40†Index】\nL56:     1. 【41†Terms defined by this specification】\nL57:     2. 【42†Terms defined by reference】\nL58:   11. 【43†References】\nL59:     1. 【44†Normative References】\nL60:     2. 【45†Non-Normative References】\nL61:   12. 【46†Property Index】\nL62: ## 1. Introduction\nL63: \nL64: This section is not normative.\nL65: \nL66: Large documents or applications (and even small ones) can contain quite a bit of CSS. Many of the values in the CSS file will be duplicate data; for example, a site may establish a color scheme and reuse three or four colors throughout the site. Altering this data can be difficult and error-prone, since it’s scattered throughout the CSS file (and possibly across multiple files), and may not be amenable to Find-and-Replace.\nL67: This module introduces a family of custom author-defined properties known collectively as 【47†custom properties】, which allow an author to assign arbitrary values to a property with an author-chosen name, and the 【48†var()】 function, which allow an author to then use those values in other properties elsewhere in the document.\nL68: This makes it easier to read large files, as seemingly-arbitrary values now have informative names, and makes editing such files much easier and less error-prone, as one only has to change the value once, in the custom property, and the change will propagate to all uses of that variable automatically.\nL69: ### 1.1. Value Definitions\nL70: \nL71: This specification follows the 【49†CSS property definition conventions†www.w3.org】 from 【50†[CSS2]】 using the 【51†value definition syntax†www.w3.org】 from 【52†[CSS-VALUES-3]】. Value types not defined in this specification are defined in CSS Values & Units 【52†[CSS-VALUES-3]】. Combination with other CSS modules may expand the definitions of these value types.\nL72: In addition to the property-specific values listed in their definitions, all properties defined in this specification also accept the 【53†CSS-wide keywords】 as their property value. For readability they have not been repeated explicitly.\nL73: ## 2. Defining Custom Properties: the --* family of properties\nL74: \nL75: This specification defines an open-ended set of properties called 【47†custom properties】, which, among other things, are used to define the 【54†substitution value】 of 【48†var()】 functions.\nL76: Name:  | --*\nL77: 【55†Value:†www.w3.org】  | 【56†<declaration-value>】【57†?】\nL78: 【58†Initial:†www.w3.org】  | the 【59†guaranteed-invalid value】\nL79: 【60†Applies to:†www.w3.org】  | all elements and all pseudo-elements (including those with restricted property lists)\nL80: 【61†Inherited:†www.w3.org】  | yes\nL81: 【62†Percentages:†www.w3.org】  | n/a\nL82: 【63†Computed value:†www.w3.org】  | specified value with variables substituted, or the 【59†guaranteed-invalid value】\nL83: 【64†Canonical order:†www.w3.org】  | per grammar\nL84: 【65†Animation type:†www.w3.org】  | discrete\nL85: User agents are expected to support this property on all media, including non-visual ones.\nL86: A custom property is any property whose name starts with two dashes (U+002D HYPHEN-MINUS), like --foo. The <custom-property-name> production corresponds to this: it’s defined as any 【66†<dashed-ident>】 (a valid 【67†identifier】 that starts with two dashes), except -- itself, which is reserved for future use by CSS. 【47†Custom properties】 are solely for use by authors and users; CSS will never give them a meaning beyond what is presented here.\nL87: \nL88: Tests\n"
